nd and it turns out um  you can see that certainly the earliest  their mowed the fewer flowers we have uh  in subsequent years okay  and the trick that we're trying to  figure out is really what are those  dates  and and with  with crocus and with the teta tets that  we just looked at  and and probably with silla siberica  sometime about the first week of june um  we we believe that it's generally safe  to do the first mowing  on many of these in-grass bulbs  and  we think that's notable because that's  about a month earlier than  than most people typically would wait  which again would be about the first  week of july when thing when all the  leaves are totally yellow  so with this research um  we're seeing that there's really not a  big difference in flowers from from a  june 9th mowing to a july 7th mowing and  and this particular graph  had been after three  mowing seasons so that this is really  quite a long-term experiment that we're  looking at here  some of them we don't think it matters  very much crocus thomas  thomas indiana's ruby giant which is the  earliest one  uh  there's there's  really may 20 the end of may is is safe  to mow these  so we're we're identifying mowing dates  for different species um  over time in the research that we're  doing  so i've got just a couple of a couple of  minutes um and if there's any questions  i see the chat are there any questions  neil in the chat  right real quick and then if not i'll  show a few more pictures  um  steve asked  uh  can the bulb planter control plant  density and spacing oh yeah good point  thank you steve uh yes it can control  plant density uh in a in a crude way  there is a gate which you can open or  close to regulate the flow of bulge out  of the hopper and so so uh yes there is  a certain amount of control for that and  that depends on the bulb size and it  depends on whether it's a mixture or not  but we can certainly we can certainly  regulate that uh to to quite a large  extent  um we've done other uh demonstrations  installations uh  especially down on long island with with  nasa and suffolk counties they've been  quite involved in this  um  you can go a little bit over the top  with some of these displays this is at  the front gate of eisenhower park in  nassau county and  these were these were leftover bulbs  that we had so we really gave them a  nice show and it's fantastic people  people really love this stuff  um  ithaca is working on a one more minute  ithaca is working on a defined  funding mechanism for individuals and  business owners  to  formally contribute uh money uh into a  bulb budget  and because we've done a lot of  plantings in downtown ithaca and garden  clubs have gotten involved service  organizations have been giving small  grants for this and and so there's  there's some possibilities coming along  and again lots of nice things uh the the  bees the bees loving the crocus other  pollinator benefits and so on  so i'm i'm probably a little bit out of  time um  i i would be happy to  see if there are any other questions  you're right on time bill um there was a  question that came in on thoughts for  using the bulb planter for planting  garlic or onion sets  oh did steve reiner's ass  he did yes well if steve if steve would  get some uh  garlic cloves to us in the fall we  should try that but i i think it would  work just fine  um and i think it would work fine for  onion sets it it does not  uh it does not plant in rose it plants  in a swath in an 18-inch wide swath and  when when the bulbs drop down  they they bounce around randomly and  it's not the case that that bulbs that  the pointy side has to go up and the  roots go down the bulb takes care of all  of that itself  so it doesn't matter so so uh  planting  planting onion sets would be just like  planting a kiana doxa it would be fine  they wouldn't be precisely placed but  they would you know again we can adjust  the the general planting density quite  nicely with the machine  that's great thank you let me ask um oh  sis  someone asked about lily's daylilies or  other late season plantings oh good  question uh we have not tried lily's and  i i think lily's would be  problematic because  of of the the way the hopper works in  the belt that drives the bulbs up to the  gate  and then the bulbs have to kind of  squish through the gate  my my fear is that lilies might get  damaged um because they they don't have  that papery tunic that a tulip or a  daffodil  or a hyacinth does and and that those  scales would get broken off i that's  that's my fear  interestingly we have tried day lilies  and i think we tried hostas as  whether this machine could be used to  plant bare root perennials  um and and and direct direct planting of  bare roots into larger landscapes is a  totally legitimate thing to do  but the difficulty with this is that  is that with when bare roots are planted  if they are planted  significantly under the ground uh if if  they're planted two or three inches deep  if the crown of the bare root is under  the ground  uh they are very stunted in their growth  and emergence  and it's definitely the case that with  bare roots you the the crown needs to be  planted right on the soil surface so  that they they get that signal of light  and and they they root and they start  growing  um  so no i don't think this has a place  with bare roots unfortunately  okay thank you bill that was an  excellent presentation  um i'm fortunate that i live in ithaca  and get to see the benefit of all these  plantings when i drive to to work every  day in the spring so thank you  then i will continue on with our last  presentation of the session which is  coping with rising greenhouse energy  costs  and unfortunately we are we are here  again so the reason i'm going to talk  about this topic is we've noticed that  especially over the last uh 12 18 months  or so energy prices have gone up again  um  we hit kind of a uh a low in energy or  decrease in energy prices especially  that first spring of covid  when people were staying at home using  less energy  fuel prices went down  and now they've they've gone up so just  in the last 12 18 months instead of  paying two dollars per gallon for fuel  oil we're paying about three and a half  dollars per gallon for fuel oil  um i'm going to talk a little bit about  strategies for reducing energy use i'll  talk first about energy audits and  benchmarking and grants that are  available then i'll talk about energy  conservation which are things that you  can do  with the structure or the equipment that  you already have  and then energy efficiency which would  be upgrades to to equipment or to fuel  to further save energy  so we'll dive right in  and one of the things i like to think  about if you're looking at saving energy  in your operation  is this energy pyramid where it's most  cost effective to start at the bottom of  the pyramid  that's where your quickest cheapest  gains and energy use can be made and  then at the top of the period are are  these more expensive  components so the the cheapest things to  do are to analyze your energy use  whether that's yourself or whether  that's through getting a free business  energy audit that's available  through nyserda for example  to analyze how you're currently using  energy and see what changes can be made  then energy conservation then new  equipment that gets used with energy  efficiency  then time of use management so that  could be say upgraded controllers so  that your  heat or your lights are  are on  when needed and not on when not needed  and then finally once you've taken  advantage of all those existing energy  changes  that that would be the time to consider  renewable energy um to to then reduce  your energy bill because you're making  your own energy such as  solar or wind for example  um so just a little bit about energy  audits this provides an overview of your  current energy usage it typically um  through a formal procedure you'll you'll  have someone come and visit  they'll do a site visit  they'll look through utility bills  ideally they'll look at three years  worth of utility bills they'll also look  at specifics for your equipment like  what type of boiler you have for your  greenhouse or furnace that you have for  your greenhouse  then based on that information they're  going to estimate energy savings or  practice changes or equipment upgrades  and then they'll also give you a  framework for using that information so  they'll help you prioritize  what is the return on investment for  upgrades so you can see what what is  most cost effective  for your operation  here's an example of an energy audit  where uh there were some recommendations  that came in  um they have an estimated payback period  the installed cost and whether the  energy being saved is electricity or  whether it's its heating fuel so  installing a higher energy efficiency  lighting system depending on the type of  system you install could have a payback  of one to ten years  installing lighting controls  at a cost of five to eight thousand  dollars per controller would have a  payback of one to three years in this  case  um and then in terms of saving on  heating fuel installing an energy  curtain to to retain heat in the  greenhouse  would cost two to five dollars per  square foot and have a one to three year  payback  upgrading the the boiler the furnace to  a condensing heating system would have a  two to five year payback and then  upgrading the glazing material i think  this particular example was uh an  infrared  locking glazing material that helps to  track trap the  long wave radiation from the sun in the  greenhouse  a related  process is called energy benchmarking  and this is this is relatively new  there's a few different energy  benchmarks out on the market um the idea  with these is is that you track your  current energy use which would again  relate to using utility bills to track  the energy that you're using  um these what these benchmarks do is  that they allow you to um compare your  energy use to anonymous peers of similar  size and technology so so besides  entering your information you get to see  how  anonymous other people  are doing as well that can help you  realize okay i'm using a lot more energy  than other  greenhouse lettuce growers or other  greenhouse flower growers let me let me  dive in and see what we're doing  differently  and then this also gives you  that a framework for making  changes over time and by by tracking  energy use over time you can track  those those energy improvements that  you've made  um we have so so i'm part of this um  research group called glaze.org glaze  stands for greenhouse lighting and  systems engineering um we're currently  working with nyserda new york state  energy research and development  authority to offer an energy benchmark  for free to 80 greenhouses in new york  state  nyserda also has  a free energy audit program  so you can go to their their website  nyserda.newyork.gov  or you can google nyserda and  agriculture energy audit and find  information about enrolling there  i think they have a little bit of  information about the energy benchmark  program too but otherwise you can go to  glaze.org  to find information about the the  database and benchmark to all  so we're fortunate in new york to have  um nyserda as a strong supporter of  these  these  energy audit and benchmarking programs  in terms of money that you could get for  making energy efficiency  upgrades  i always recommend reaching out first to  your utility company oftentimes utility  companies will have incentives for  energy efficiency  so they they can give you money back  for the improvements that you make  and then  we also have the usda through the  natural resources conservation service  through the nrcs  there's a program that they have every  year that's called reap  renewable energy and energy efficiency  and what this program does  it actually provides 25 that should say  grant so you get  you get a quarter of the cost of energy  efficiency upgrades paid for um and then  they also have 75 percent loan  guarantees as well to help make sure  that you can get the uh the money if you  need a loan um to make those upgrades  um for the the nrcs grants i always  recommend that people check with their  county office first about the current  programs that are available um and the  the county office folks sometimes can  offer you support in terms of filling  out the application or getting the  information that you need  so then let me move on to these  energy conservation and energy  efficiency measures  i get this question a lot why does it  cost so much to heat a greenhouse and  it's a valid question  greenhouses unfortunately don't have the  same amount of insulation as our homes  because we're trying to let in as much  sunlight as possible so we typically  have glass or  plastic or polycarbonate  and that's made to be really good at  letting light through but it's not very  good at trapping heat  we can look at  the ability to to trap in heat as the  insulation value or the r value  and so  here a larger number is higher  and  one of the big things to to notice here  is that a two inch  foam board has about 10 times the  insulation value as a single layer of  plastic or a single layer of glass  and then six inch thick  fiberglass insulation has about 20 times  the insulated value so if you think  about heating a thousand square foot  house  it's going to cost 10 to 20 times more  to heat  that same square footage of a greenhouse  one of the one of the things that we can  adopt is using double layer either  plastic or polycarbonate  and that does improve the r value so  that that does improve the  reducing heat loss  in that case the expense is that it  reduces the the transmission of light  somewhat so a single layer of plastic or  glass let through about 90 percent of  the light a double layer of plastic or  glass will let through about 80 percent  of the light  so energy conservation steps are simple  actions that you can take to reduce your  energy with the structure and equipment  that you already have  um so these could be things like furnace  maintenance they could be  actually scheduling your crop so that  when you  open up your greenhouse and start  heating it that your that your  greenhouse is full  and not half empty  it could also relate to reducing the run  time of equipment so if there's  maybe lights that are on in the  headhouse of your greenhouse you could  have those on a sensor so that those  lights turn on only when there's  actually people working in that head  house for example  um one of the the most common energy  conservation measures that we recommend  is reducing air infiltration which  essentially means sealing the leaks in  your greenhouse  so any kind of leak is going to let the  warm air out of your greenhouse and let  the cold air in  and so that could relate to patching  holes in your your polyethylene plastic  that you have  sealing up other leaks actually weather  stripping doors  so if your door has a 1 8 inch gap under  the door  you can lose two dollars worth of heat  per hour so imagine that just putting  weather stripping on the door that's  going to have a return on investment of  like a day  then another measure that we recommend  is installing perimeter insulation um  this would have to be below the the  bench height because we want your crops  to get as much light as possible um but  below the bench height you could install  one to two inch foam board ideally that  would be sunk one to two  feet into the ground so that you don't  have incursion of those cold  temperatures up through the the  florentii or greenhouse  so these air infiltration methods can  save you five to fifteen percent of your  heating energy bill  uh then i want to move on to talking  about energy efficiency measures so  these are using less energy by upgrading  your equipment or switching to more  energy efficient equipment  so some of the equipment upgrades could  be replacing your old furnace with a  more efficient one replacing your your  lights with more efficient ones adding  energy curtains to retain heat um as  well as considering different fuel  sources so  if you you might have access to a more  cost effective  fuel source  so energy curtains  these are uh  retractable close-weaved curtains so  these are often made of an aluminum type  of material  they typically serve a dual purpose so  that you can get curtains that provide  say 50 shading  and you can use them in the hot summer  days when you want to to keep your  greenhouse cooler  and they work by deflecting some of the  sunlight away from the greenhouse at  that point  but then if you deploy these at  nighttime so we'll close them at sunset  they can reduce heat loss at night by 30  to 50 percent  and the nighttime is really when our  greenhouses lose the most heat because  we do get a fair bit of solar radiation  gain during the day  so  over all these curtains can save  sometimes  20 to 40 percent um they can reduce your  heat loss by 20 to 40 percent overall  the cost is about two to five dollars  per square foot so they typically have a  return on investment of about two to  three years  um you can even consider multiple  curtains which give you even better heat  retention and that works because you  have the curtains themselves  and their heat retention properties but  then if you deploy multiple curtains you  get a layer of air that acts as as an  insulative layer  this was a greenhouse that i happened to  visit in japan where energy costs are  about three times higher than they are  in new york  um and you'll notice that in this the  strawberry greenhouse they actually had  three layers of energy curtains um so  they could really reduce their their  heat loss during the night time  another energy efficiency measure would  be to look at um replacing old boilers  and furnaces with new condensing boilers  or heaters  and the reason that these help is is  older boilers that are not condensing  lose a lot of heat through water vapor  so when you when you burn a fuel source  when you burn a fossil fuel um a lot of  hot water vapor is created and that  water vapor is at 400 to 500 degrees  fahrenheit and typically it's just lost  through this the stack of the the  furnace  and so these condensing boilers what  they do is they add a heat exchanger  that recaptures a lot of the  heat from that high temperature water  so then if you if you feel the exhaust  not that i recommend it it should  actually be be  room temperature or warm not hot to the  touch because a lot of that heat has  been regained  so you can get boilers  and furnaces that have an energy  efficiency of 90 to 95 percent instead  of the older ones which would have 70 to  80 percent efficiency  then um i always like to show this chart  to look at different fuel sources  this this there's a table that's  available online that's put together by  john bartok who's a retired greenhouse  engineer from the university of  connecticut  and in the table you can plug in the  price  for different fuel sources and then it  calculates the cost per million btus  so then we can look at these fuel  sources in kind of an apples to apples  way  and natural gas is by and large our  cheapest fuel source so if you're  fortunate enough to be on natural gas  that would be the the cheapest way to  heat your greenhouse  in this scenario about fourteen dollars  per million btus of heat  and a lot of them are rural growers  unfortunately are on uh you either have  to get propane or fuel oil and so your  cost per million btus can be can be  twice as much  so biomass fuel sources like  cord wood or wood pellets could actually  save you a lot of money for your fuel  bill especially if your alternative is  propane or fuel oil  some of the considerations that you  should take into account if you're  looking at alternative fuels is they  make the most sense if you heat through  much of the winter  um so if you're only heating in like  march and april  um it may not make sense to to upgrade  equipment or change your fuel source  because you're you're  have too many years to see the return on  investment  you also want to consider using a  material that's available locally or  available inexpensively  it should be something that you can  source consistently from year to year  you do need to think about having enough  storage space on site like for the cord  wood  or for a wood pellet hopper you also  need to think about labor for material  handling  i visited some some large greenhouses  that switched to biomass heat um and  they essentially budgeted for one person  working full-time  um to handle the to either like prepare  the wood load hoppers um and dispose of  of ash  so think about ash disposal  and then um in the end this has to make  sense economically considering all of  the costs so the cost for a new furnace  the cost for labor the cost for space  the cost for the fuel source itself  and as i mentioned our  upgrades to heating systems make the  most sense if we're heating for  throughout the whole winter so our our  months where we have the largest number  of heating degree days  are december january and february  um and then  that  really goes down dramatically once we  start getting to the spring months  installing an integrated  control system or or a computer climate  control system can save five to fifteen  percent of your energy savings over  thermostat control  so kind of the low-tech way that we  control heating and venting decisions in  the greenhouse is with a thermostat and  often there's a separate thermostat for  the the furnace or boiler and a separate  thermostat for the vents and what we  find is that sometimes if their settings  are not set appropriately or if the  thermostat is not located in a area that  is representative of the the whole  greenhouse temperature sometimes we  get into this unwanted situation where  greenhouse is both heating and venting  at the same time which can lead to  wasted heat  so what an integrated controller does is  it makes  all of these decisions  and it  takes into account additional  information so if we're deciding to heat  a greenhouse  it's controlling the vents the heat  they typically also have an outdoor  weather station so they can  track how much solar radiation is coming  in and so your greenhouse might be  calling for heat but then the computer  system realizes that the sun has come  out and our greenhouse is going to heat  up quickly with solar radiation so that  the integrated control system could  decide to delay heating for example  these systems can also log data so that  if something goes wrong you can go back  over time and say oh what happened  two months ago which caused my crop to  really go downhill fast  um and the the last a few slides that i  want to spend relates to greenhouse  lighting  um and so one of the points is that  greenhouse lighting has significantly  improved over the last 25 years or so in  terms of its energy efficiency  the way that we measure the the energy  efficiency of lights  is their um light output which is the  number of photosynthetic photons that  they put out  per joule of electricity and then again  a higher number is a better number  so what we find is that our older high  pressure sodium fixtures  had a lower  light output per energy use  even then a newer high pressure sodium  fixtures so  if you haven't upgraded your high  pressure sodium lights in the last 10 or  20 years this definitely deserves a  second look so nowadays we have double  ended  bulbs the filament connects on both  sides of the bulb and they also have an  electronic ballast instead of a magnetic  ballast  and those offer already  something like 65 70 percent energy  savings over old high pressure sodium  lights  then the interesting thing to me is that  leds have been much talked about in  terms of energy efficiency  in 2014 the best leds were not any  better in energy efficiency than the  best high pressure sodium lights but  then since that time over the last eight  years we've made really dramatic  increases in energy use efficiency so by  2016 they were 40 percent better  now there's some leds that are even  almost 100 percent better than high  pressure sodium lights  so if you haven't looked at greenhouse  lighting for a while um it could deserve  a second look especially if you  have crops in your greenhouse  again in like december january february  when we have the least amount of  sunlight outdoors  i get this question from growers a lot  which is which leds are going to be  reliable which ones how do i find what  their specifications are how do i know  what manufacturers to look to  um and there's this really nice resource  that's called the dlc or the design  lights consortium  you can find them at designlights.org  they maintain a list that's called their  horticultural lighting qualified  products list  and what this list does is  they actually have to have their  fixtures certified to be on the list  that they meet a minimum energy  efficiency which means they're they're  at least a bit better than high pressure  sodium lights  they maintain their light output for 36  000 hours so we rate fixtures in terms  of what we call q90 which is the number  of hours until the light output  degrades to 90 of the original so  they're 10 dimmer at that point um and  and they need to last at least 36 000  hours  um then the driver lifetime needs to be  at least 50 000 hours and the fan life  time of at least 50 000 hours and the  manufacturer has to supply  at least a five year warranty  so you can find  postings at designlights.org you can  also contact me i'm happy to work  through those questions with the growers  in new york state and and help you make  your lighting decisions  um  and then it seems like all i'm doing  today is mentioning different websites  um  do feel free to contact me in person and  i'm happy to  um to walk you through the different  resources that are out there um this is  another research group that i'm part of  port lamp so lampstart stands for  lighting approaches to maximize profit  and they have lighting calculator tools  one of them is really slick in that it  takes into account your location in the  us so you can input your zip code and it  reads off of um  10-year weather data so we know how much  light you get on average  every day of the year  so you can input specific things related  to your greenhouse the percent of light  that enters your greenhouse the target  light level for your crop the type of  the energy efficiency of the light  fixture that you have  your price for electricity and then your  greenhouse area  and then this will calculate um  this is in dollars per acre the cost of  lighting your greenhouse  so very expensive in november december  january and february and much lower on  the other months  um so this is this is a really nice  resource if you're looking at say two  different lights you can plug in  information on the energy efficiency of  the different lighting fixtures and  calculate  what the potential energy savings would  be  oh i do want to mention um  adaptive lighting control so so once you  have the lights installed in your  greenhouse  uh then the question is how do you make  decisions about when the lights turn on  and off  um and um  of course the less technical ways to do  that are just to manually flip a switch  to turn the lights on and off when you  as the grower think that it's it's dark  outside and they need light other  growers will put them on timers  um  but the timers don't take into account  whether it's a sunny day or a cloudy day  um so the most energy efficient  uh practice is to use adaptive lighting  control  where um that that c