 what this program does  it actually provides 25 that should say  grant so you get  you get a quarter of the cost of energy  efficiency upgrades paid for um and then  they also have 75 percent loan  guarantees as well to help make sure  that you can get the uh the money if you  need a loan um to make those upgrades  um for the the nrcs grants i always  recommend that people check with their  county office first about the current  programs that are available um and the  the county office folks sometimes can  offer you support in terms of filling  out the application or getting the  information that you need  so then let me move on to these  energy conservation and energy  efficiency measures  i get this question a lot why does it  cost so much to heat a greenhouse and  it's a valid question  greenhouses unfortunately don't have the  same amount of insulation as our homes  because we're trying to let in as much  sunlight as possible so we typically  have glass or  plastic or polycarbonate  and that's made to be really good at  letting light through but it's not very  good at trapping heat  we can look at  the ability to to trap in heat as the  insulation value or the r value  and so  here a larger number is higher  and  one of the big things to to notice here  is that a two inch  foam board has about 10 times the  insulation value as a single layer of  plastic or a single layer of glass  and then six inch thick  fiberglass insulation has about 20 times  the insulated value so if you think  about heating a thousand square foot  house  it's going to cost 10 to 20 times more  to heat  that same square footage of a greenhouse  one of the one of the things that we can  adopt is using double layer either  plastic or polycarbonate  and that does improve the r value so  that that does improve the  reducing heat loss  in that case the expense is that it  reduces the the transmission of light  somewhat so a single layer of plastic or  glass let through about 90 percent of  the light a double layer of plastic or  glass will let through about 80 percent  of the light  so energy conservation steps are simple  actions that you can take to reduce your  energy with the structure and equipment  that you already have  um so these could be things like furnace  maintenance they could be  actually scheduling your crop so that  when you  open up your greenhouse and start  heating it that your that your  greenhouse is full  and not half empty  it could also relate to reducing the run  time of equipment so if there's  maybe lights that are on in the  headhouse of your greenhouse you could  have those on a sensor so that those  lights turn on only when there's  actually people working in that head  house for example  um one of the the most common energy  conservation measures that we recommend  is reducing air infiltration which  essentially means sealing the leaks in  your greenhouse  so any kind of leak is going to let the  warm air out of your greenhouse and let  the cold air in  and so that could relate to patching  holes in your your polyethylene plastic  that you have  sealing up other leaks actually weather  stripping doors  so if your door has a 1 8 inch gap under  the door  you can lose two dollars worth of heat  per hour so imagine that just putting  weather stripping on the door that's  going to have a return on investment of  like a day  then another measure that we recommend  is installing perimeter insulation um  this would have to be below the the  bench height because we want your crops  to get as much light as possible um but  below the bench height you could install  one to two inch foam board ideally that  would be sunk one to two  feet into the ground so that you don't  have incursion of those cold  temperatures up through the the  florentii or greenhouse  so these air infiltration methods can  save you five to fifteen percent of your  heating energy bill  uh then i want to move on to talking  about energy efficiency measures so  these are using less energy by upgrading  your equipment or switching to more  energy efficient equipment  so some of the equipment upgrades could  be replacing your old furnace with a  more efficient one replacing your your  lights with more efficient ones adding  energy curtains to retain heat um as  well as considering different fuel  sources so  if you you might have access to a more  cost effective  fuel source  so energy curtains  these are uh  retractable close-weaved curtains so  these are often made of an aluminum type  of material  they typically serve a dual purpose so  that you can get curtains that provide  say 50 shading  and you can use them in the hot summer  days when you want to to keep your  greenhouse cooler  and they work by deflecting some of the  sunlight away from the greenhouse at  that point  but then if you deploy these at  nighttime so we'll close them at sunset  they can reduce heat loss at night by 30  to 50 percent  and the nighttime is really when our  greenhouses lose the most heat because  we do get a fair bit of solar radiation  gain during the day  so  over all these curtains can save  sometimes  20 to 40 percent um they can reduce your  heat loss by 20 to 40 percent overall  the cost is about two to five dollars  per square foot so they typically have a  return on investment of about two to  three years  um you can even consider multiple  curtains which give you even better heat  retention and that works because you  have the curtains themselves  and their heat retention properties but  then if you deploy multiple curtains you  get a layer of air that acts as as an  insulative layer  this was a greenhouse that i happened to  visit in japan where energy costs are  about three times higher than they are  in new york  um and you'll notice that in this the  strawberry greenhouse they actually had  three layers of energy curtains um so  they could really reduce their their  heat loss during the night time  another energy efficiency measure would  be to look at um replacing old boilers  and furnaces with new condensing boilers  or heaters  and the reason that these help is is  older boilers that are not condensing  lose a lot of heat through water vapor  so when you when you burn a fuel source  when you burn a fossil fuel um a lot of  hot water vapor is created and that  water vapor is at 400 to 500 degrees  fahrenheit and typically it's just lost  through this the stack of the the  furnace  and so these condensing boilers what  they do is they add a heat exchanger  that recaptures a lot of the  heat from that high temperature water  so then if you if you feel the exhaust  not that i recommend it it should  actually be be  room temperature or warm not hot to the  touch because a lot of that heat has  been regained  so you can get boilers  and furnaces that have an energy  efficiency of 90 to 95 percent instead  of the older ones which would have 70 to  80 percent efficiency  then um i always like to show this chart  to look at different fuel sources  this this there's a table that's  available online that's put together by  john bartok who's a retired greenhouse  engineer from the university of  connecticut  and in the table you can plug in the  price  for different fuel sources and then it  calculates the cost per million btus  so then we can look at these fuel  sources in kind of an apples to apples  way  and natural gas is by and large our  cheapest fuel source so if you're  fortunate enough to be on natural gas  that would be the the cheapest way to  heat your greenhouse  in this scenario about fourteen dollars  per million btus of heat  and a lot of them are rural growers  unfortunately are on uh you either have  to get propane or fuel oil and so your  cost per million btus can be can be  twice as much  so biomass fuel sources like  cord wood or wood pellets could actually  save you a lot of money for your fuel  bill especially if your alternative is  propane or fuel oil  some of the considerations that you  should take into account if you're  looking at alternative fuels is they  make the most sense if you heat through  much of the winter  um so if you're only heating in like  march and april  um it may not make sense to to upgrade  equipment or change your fuel source  because you're you're  have too many years to see the return on  investment  you also want to consider using a  material that's available locally or  available inexpensively  it should be something that you can  source consistently from year to year  you do need to think about having enough  storage space on site like for the cord  wood  or for a wood pellet hopper you also  need to think about labor for material  handling  i visited some some large greenhouses  that switched to biomass heat um and  they essentially budgeted for one person  working full-time  um to handle the to either like prepare  the wood load hoppers um and dispose of  of ash  so think about ash disposal  and then um in the end this has to make  sense economically considering all of  the costs so the cost for a new furnace  the cost for labor the cost for space  the cost for the fuel source itself  and as i mentioned our  upgrades to heating systems make the  most sense if we're heating for  throughout the whole winter so our our  months where we have the largest number  of heating degree days  are december january and february  um and then  that  really goes down dramatically once we  start getting to the spring months  installing an integrated  control system or or a computer climate  control system can save five to fifteen  percent of your energy savings over  thermostat control  so kind of the low-tech way that we  control heating and venting decisions in  the greenhouse is with a thermostat and  often there's a separate thermostat for  the the furnace or boiler and a separate  thermostat for the vents and what we  find is that sometimes if their settings  are not set appropriately or if the  thermostat is not located in a area that  is representative of the the whole  greenhouse temperature sometimes we  get into this unwanted situation where  greenhouse is both heating and venting  at the same time which can lead to  wasted heat  so what an integrated controller does is  it makes  all of these decisions  and it  takes into account additional  information so if we're deciding to heat  a greenhouse  it's controlling the vents the heat  they typically also have an outdoor  weather station so they can  track how much solar radiation is coming  in and so your greenhouse might be  calling for heat but then the computer  system realizes that the sun has come  out and our greenhouse is going to heat  up quickly with solar radiation so that  the integrated control system could  decide to delay heating for example  these systems can also log data so that  if something goes wrong you can go back  over time and say oh what happened  two months ago which caused my crop to  really go downhill fast  um and the the last a few slides that i  want to spend relates to greenhouse  lighting  um and so one of the points is that  greenhouse lighting has significantly  improved over the last 25 years or so in  terms of its energy efficiency  the way that we measure the the energy  efficiency of lights  is their um light output which is the  number of photosynthetic photons that  they put out  per joule of electricity and then again  a higher number is a better number  so what we find is that our older high  pressure sodium fixtures  had a lower  light output per energy use  even then a newer high pressure sodium  fixtures so  if you haven't upgraded your high  pressure sodium lights in the last 10 or  20 years this definitely deserves a  second look so nowadays we have double  ended  bulbs the filament connects on both  sides of the bulb and they also have an  electronic ballast instead of a magnetic  ballast  and those offer already  something like 65 70 percent energy  savings over old high pre